<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ephesus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ayasoluk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ephesus was an ancient Greek city on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, near modern Selçuk in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, famed for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-artemis">Temple of Artemis</a>, one of the Seven Wonders of the World. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> it was the capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and an early centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. As its harbour silted up, life moved to the nearby hill of Ayasoluk, which the Aydinids took in the early 14th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ephesus.jpg" alt="Ephesus" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benh LIEU SONG · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ephesus_Celsus_Library_Fa%C3%A7ade.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 569 John of <b>Ephesus</b> mentioned it for the first time, and a late 6th-century Greek document from Byzantine Egypt recording the sale of an Alodian slave girl confirms its existence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ephesus</b> lay on the west coast of Asia Minor near the mouth of the Cayster River, on a bay that has since silted up and left its ruins several kilometres inland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Croesus attacked <b>Ephesus</b> and brought all the Greek cities of the coast under tribute.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tyrian annals that the Jewish historian Josephus quoted from Menander of <b>Ephesus</b> say that he enlarged the island city and rebuilt its temples.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>…the hymn was usually placed in the 6th or 7th century; Leena Peltomaa argued from its theology for the 5th century, and most scholars now favour an early date, somewhere between the Councils of <b>Ephesus</b> and Chalcedon or, in another view, the later 5th or early 6th century.</blockquote><a href="/songs/akathist">Akathist Hymn</a></li><li><blockquote>His teaching on the two natures of Christ was condemned at <b>Ephesus</b> in 431, and the Church of the East that kept his name spread as far as China.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-10">10 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
